  "courseSlug": "mastering-linux",
  "courseTitle": "Mastering Linux: The Comprehensive Guide",
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "39570502",
      "title": "Unsetting Variables",
      "lectureOrder": 419,
      "videoOrder": 387,
      "sourceHash": "8a63af1df4372455b734cb9302b02a10c372d552fa2b47003774180be9bb60ac",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 0.61,
          "end": 5.11,
          "en": "Let's now have a look at how we can delete or unset a variable in bash.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 5.96,
          "end": 9.35,
          "en": "If we want to unset a variable in bash most often.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 9.35,
          "end": 10.67,
          "en": "Actually we never do that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 10.67,
          "end": 12.26,
          "en": "We just use a different variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 12.26,
          "end": 15.92,
          "en": "Then the reason is that bash scripts are not that long anyway.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 15.92,
          "end": 23.18,
          "en": "We have seen the guides, the guidelines Bash script should be a maximum of 100 lines, preferably even",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 23.18,
          "end": 23.75,
          "en": "shorter.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 23.75,
          "end": 28.64,
          "en": "And then it's usually okay to just have a variable that's that sticks around.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 29,
          "end": 34.52,
          "en": "The reason is that once our bash script is finished, our whole bash process is removed from memory",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 34.52,
          "end": 35.09,
          "en": "anyway.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 35.09,
          "end": 38.75,
          "en": "So we can also just not unset a variable at all.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 39.86,
          "end": 45.74,
          "en": "Another method is that we don't really unset the variable, but that we just assign an empty value to",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 45.74,
          "end": 46.64,
          "en": "a variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 46.67,
          "end": 53.24,
          "en": "This would not remove the variable, it would still exist, but the variable would be set to the empty",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 53.27,
          "end": 54.01,
          "en": "string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 54.02,
          "end": 58.04,
          "en": "Why is this considered deleting or unsetting a variable?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 58.07,
          "end": 62.51,
          "en": "The reason is that if I try to access a variable that doesn't exist.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 63.39,
          "end": 64.02,
          "en": "Here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 64.02,
          "end": 68.08,
          "en": "I'm now trying to access a variable that is not existing in my shell.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 68.1,
          "end": 70.28999999999999,
          "en": "It just happens to be called not exists.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 70.32,
          "end": 74.65,
          "en": "We can see here it's just an empty string that's being filled in there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 74.67,
          "end": 81.12,
          "en": "So if a variable doesn't exist, it almost always behaves like an empty string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 81.12,
          "end": 89.49,
          "en": "I just say almost because there are a few minor exceptions, but it almost behaves like an empty string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 89.88,
          "end": 93.50999999999999,
          "en": "Let's create one of those empty vars though.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 93.50999999999999,
          "end": 98.61,
          "en": "So here we now have this empty var that's set to the empty string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 98.61,
          "end": 104.58,
          "en": "And now I could access this variable and we can see it's exactly the same output.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 104.82,
          "end": 110.03,
          "en": "The way we can differentiate between those would be with the declare minus p command.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 110.03999999999999,
          "end": 117.06,
          "en": "And if I would now query the empty var, we would see that this variable is indeed a variable that just",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 117.06,
          "end": 119.52000000000001,
          "en": "happens to contain the empty string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 119.52000000000001,
          "end": 125.44,
          "en": "But this other variable, this not exists variable that doesn't exist on my system.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 126.52,
          "end": 129.28,
          "en": "There we can see that this variable is not found.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 129.28,
          "end": 131.11,
          "en": "So there is a difference.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 131.11,
          "end": 135.1,
          "en": "And technically this is not enough to remove a variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 135.13,
          "end": 141.49,
          "en": "To really remove a variable, we need to use the unset command and this then completely removes the",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 141.49,
          "end": 142.3,
          "en": "variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 142.54,
          "end": 144.43,
          "en": "Let's also confirm this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 144.43,
          "end": 152.26,
          "en": "I could now unset my empty var and now go back to my declare minus p command and then we can see also",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 152.26,
          "end": 157.21,
          "en": "this variable now cannot be found because now it has been removed completely.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 158.51,
          "end": 162.68,
          "en": "This would be the way to go if we really want to remove a variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 162.71,
          "end": 168.38,
          "en": "But those two other ways that I mentioned here, they are also used quite often.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 168.38,
          "end": 175.97,
          "en": "But this would be the preferred way if we really want to remove a variable that we also actually remove",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 175.97,
          "end": 178.73,
          "en": "it and not just set it to the empty string.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
